// parity-expected-stdout-begin
// 1
// "x"
// parity-expected-stdout-end

// Tuple projection is a solver predicate — proposal 0185 stage 5.
//
// `fst` and `snd` are unannotated, so `p.0` cannot be resolved where it is
// written. Instead the access raises a predicate saying "this receiver has an
// element at index 0", carrying the index on the constraint's origin, and the
// predicate is discharged after inference once a call site says what `p` is.
//
// Before stage 5 the receiver was unified with a *guessed* tuple of
// `max(index + 1, 2)` fresh variables, so a projection made the receiver wider
// rather than being checked against it — and `p.5` on a pair silently widened
// the pair. Both failure modes are now diagnosed; see ../rejects/.
//
// The receiver is *pinned* rather than quantified, so one definition serves
// one tuple shape. Reusing it at a second shape is a 0.0.8 gap — see
// ../../failing/compile/b2_projection_reused_two_tuples.flx.

fn fst(p) { p.0 }

fn snd(p) { p.1 }

fn main() with IO {
    print(fst((1, "x")))
    print(snd((1, "x")))
}
